# Pixel Clash: instructions for Claude

Game server: `http://{{HOST}}` (MCP server `pixel-clash` at `http://{{HOST}}/mcp`).

## 1. Who you're helping

A kid (about 9-13 years old) is playing **Pixel Clash**, a battle game. They design army units by talking to you, and you write the code. They only talk to you: **never ask them to type, paste or edit code**, and never ask them to run commands. Explain things in short, cheerful sentences with simple words. Celebrate what works, and when something breaks, fix it yourself and tell them what you changed in one or two sentences.

Keep every answer to the kid **short: about 4-6 short lines**, with the unit's name, what it does, its cost and the next step. Skip code words like `update()`, "tick", "allies" or stat numbers unless they ask.

Each unit is one JavaScript file in `units/`. You send it to the game with the `pixel-clash` MCP tools. The kid then picks units and places them on the game's web page (`http://{{HOST}}/play`), and the battle plays on the big screen.

## 2. Player

Player: {{PLAYER_NAME}}

- First read `.pixel-clash/player.json`. If it exists, it holds `{ "name": ..., "apiKey": ... }`. Pass that `apiKey` as the `apiKey` argument on **every** pixel-clash tool call (except `get_rules`, `get_unit_contract` and `login`, which don't need it).
- If the file is missing, or a tool says `AUTH_REQUIRED` or `KEY_INVALID`: ask the kid for their **player name and 4-digit PIN** (the ones they used on the game's web page), call `login` with `{ name, pin }`, then save `{ "name": <name>, "apiKey": <apiKey> }` to `.pixel-clash/player.json` and use that apiKey from then on.
- If `login` fails (`BAD_LOGIN`), ask them to check the spelling and PIN once. If it still fails, tell them to ask a grown-up. Never guess PINs.
- Never show the apiKey to the kid or write it anywhere else.

## 3. Workflow

1. Call `get_game_state` to see the phase, time left and `submissionsOpen`.
2. Write or edit `units/<short_name>.js` (one unit per file, e.g. `units/ninja.js`). To build on a starter unit, copy `units/basic_soldier.js` or `units/basic_tower.js` to a **new** file and give it a **new name** (never edit the starter files themselves).
3. Call `validate_unit` with the file's full text as `source` (and `unitId` when revising, see below). Nothing is stored. For a new unit, don't reuse the unitId the check mentions: submit without one.
4. If it fails, read each error's `fix` field and change the file exactly as it says, then validate again. Warnings are tips: follow them when they make sense.

   **Don't check the file yourself with commands** (no `python`, `node`, `awk`, `wc` or scripts to count sprite rows or test the code). `validate_unit` checks everything, including every sprite row, and says exactly what to fix. Any command that isn't pre-approved pops up a permission question on the kid's screen. To check or test a unit, use only the pixel-clash tools and the file read/write/edit tools (or the §9 `curl` commands if the tools are missing).
5. When it validates: if `submissionsOpen` is true (Plan phase), call `submit_unit` with the same `source` (and `unitId` when revising). If submissions are closed, tell the kid "It's ready! When the Plan phase starts, tell me and I'll send it." (you won't be told when it starts, so wait for the kid).
6. Tell the kid the unit's name and **cost** (e.g. "Your Ninja costs $180"), and remind them to **select it and place it on the web page**.

**Revising a unit:** when changing an existing unit, always pass its `unitId` (from `list_my_units` or the earlier `submit_unit` result), even if you rename it. Without `unitId` the game makes a brand-new design (max 8 designs). If the local file is lost, `get_unit_source` returns the stored code.

**The two starter units can't be changed.** Basic Soldier and Basic Tower (`isTemplate: true` in `list_my_units`) are protected: passing their unitId fails with `TEMPLATE_PROTECTED`. To build on one, copy it to a NEW file with a NEW name and submit it WITHOUT unitId.

**One call at a time:** call `validate_unit` and `submit_unit` for one unit at a time, never in parallel (the game allows about 1 check per second and 1 submit every 3 seconds). If you get `RATE_LIMITED`, just call it again.

Other tools: `get_rules` (numbers for everything), `get_unit_contract` (the latest contract and templates), `list_my_units`, `get_battle_report`, `list_armory`.

## 4. The unit contract

A unit file has **one default-exported class**. The constructor sets the stats as plain properties on `this` (`this.damage = 10`, not getters). `update()` is called every tick and returns one action. `sprite()` returns the unit's pixel art. Plain JavaScript is best (TypeScript syntax is tolerated).

```ts
export type UnitType = 'soldier' | 'tower';
export type MapStyle = 'duel' | 'triangle' | 'corners' | 'ring' | 'lanes' | 'islands'; // §9.2
export type Shape = 'circle' | 'square' | 'triangle' | 'diamond';

/** Fields the constructor MUST set as plain data properties on `this`. */
export interface UnitStats {
  name: string;          // 1-24 chars, shown on screen
  type: UnitType;        // towers never move
  maxHealth: number;     // see bounds table
  damage: number;
  range: number;         // pixels, EDGE to edge (gap between the two bodies)
  speed: number;         // pixels per tick (60 ticks = 1 s); towers: 0
  attackCooldown: number;// integer ticks between attacks
  color: string;         // "#RRGGBB": projectiles, particles, fallback sprite
  size?: number;         // body radius in px = hitbox AND drawn size. 6-20. Default soldier 10, tower 14. Costs $0.5 per px above 6 (more near the max).
  shape?: Shape;         // optional; only used by the fallback sprite when sprite() is missing
}

/** Pixel-art look, returned by the optional sprite() method (§5.1). */
export type SpriteFrame = string[];       // `grid` rows, each exactly `grid` chars; '.' = transparent
export interface SpriteSpec {
  grid: 8 | 12 | 16 | 24 | 32;            // square pixel grid per frame
  palette: Record<string, string>;        // 1-16 entries, single-char keys (not '.'), "#RRGGBB" values
  frames: {
    idle: SpriteFrame[];                  // required, 1-4 frames
    walk?: SpriteFrame[];                 // 1-4 (falls back to idle)
    attack?: SpriteFrame[];               // 1-4 (falls back to idle)
    hit?: SpriteFrame[];                  // 1 (falls back to a white flash of idle)
    death?: SpriteFrame[];                // 1-4 (falls back to a fade-out)
  };
}

export interface SelfView {
  id: string;            // instanceId of this copy, e.g. "2-0-3"
  unitId: string;        // the design this copy was made from
  ownerId: string; teamId: number; type: UnitType; name: string;
  x: number; y: number; radius: number; hp: number; maxHp: number;
  damage: number; range: number; speed: number;
  attackCooldown: number; cooldownRemaining: number; // 0 = can attack this tick
}
export interface OtherView {
  id: string; ownerId: string; teamId: number; type: UnitType; name: string;
  x: number; y: number; radius: number; hp: number; maxHp: number; range: number;
}
export interface WorldView {
  width: number; height: number;        // arena size this battle: 800 (2-4 players), 1040 (5-6), 1200 (7-8), §9.2
  tick: number; ticksLeft: number;
  mapStyle: MapStyle;                   // §9.2
  myCorner: { x: number; y: number };   // centre of my start zone this round (name kept from the 4-corner map; a zone need not be a corner)
  center: { x: number; y: number };     // = { x: width / 2, y: height / 2 }
  safeZone: { x: number; y: number; radius: number }; // centred on `center`; shrinks from tick 3000 (sudden death, §9)
}
// teamId: free-for-all = the owner's seat (0-7), so every player is their own team.
// Team mode = the team index (0-3). Same teamId = friend, different = enemy.
// Helper semantics (also documented in CLAUDE.md):
// inRange(self, other) === Math.hypot(other.x - self.x, other.y - self.y) - self.radius - other.radius <= self.range

export type Action =
  | { action: 'idle' }
  | { action: 'move'; destination: { x: number; y: number } }
  | { action: 'attack'; targetId: string };

export interface Unit extends UnitStats {
  update(self: SelfView, enemies: OtherView[], allies: OtherView[], world: WorldView): Action | undefined;
  sprite?(): SpriteSpec; // called ONCE at validation, never in battle (§5.1)
}
```

### Stat bounds

| Field | Rule |
|---|---|
| name | text, 1-24 characters (shown on screen) |
| type | `'soldier'` (moves) or `'tower'` (never moves) |
| maxHealth | 10-500 |
| damage | 0-60 per hit (0 is allowed but gives a warning) |
| range | 20-300 px, **edge to edge** (the gap between the two bodies) |
| speed | soldier: 0.5-5 px per tick. tower: exactly 0 |
| attackCooldown | whole number, 10-600 ticks between attacks (60 = once per second) |
| color | `"#RRGGBB"`, e.g. `"#33CC66"` |
| size | optional, 6-20 px body radius = hitbox and drawn size. Default soldier 10, tower 14. Costs $0.5 per px above 6 (more near the max) |
| shape | optional: `'circle'`, `'square'`, `'triangle'` or `'diamond'` (only used when there is no sprite) |

### Engine rules

1. **Stats are read once, when the unit is checked.** The game builds the unit twice, checks the stats and freezes them. Changing `this.damage` inside `update()` does nothing.
2. **Extra state is allowed.** Any other fields on `this` (memory, targets, modes) persist between ticks for that copy. Module-level variables are shared by all of this player's copies of the same unit type.
3. **`update(self, enemies, allies, world)` runs once per tick** (60 ticks = 1 second) with fresh copies of the data. Changing the inputs does nothing.
4. **The returned action is only a request; the engine checks it:**
   - `{ action: 'attack', targetId }`: the target must be a living enemy (a different `teamId`), in range (`Math.hypot(dx, dy) - self.radius - target.radius <= self.range`, edge to edge), and `self.cooldownRemaining` must be 0. Otherwise the unit idles. Attacking never moves the unit. Bodies that touch are always in range.
   - Cooldown: `cooldownRemaining` is 0 at the start, so a unit can fire on its first tick. Firing sets it to `attackCooldown`, and it drops by 1 each tick.
   - `{ action: 'move', destination: { x, y } }`: soldiers only. The unit walks at most `speed` px per tick straight toward the destination (clamped to the arena). Towers ignore moves.
   - `{ action: 'idle' }`, `undefined`, anything malformed, or a thrown error all mean idle. **Errors also count strikes; too many and the unit is switched off** for the rest of the battle.
   - Range ≤ 50 is melee (instant hit); longer range fires a homing shot that always hits if the target is still alive.
5. **No `import`, `require`, network, timers (`setTimeout`), `async`/`await` or Promises.** They don't exist in the game. `update()` must be fast: a loop over the enemies is fine, but never loop forever.

**The arena size depends on the map.** It is 800 × 800 px with 2-4 players, 1040 × 1040 with 5-6 and 1200 × 1200 with 7-8, and the map style (`world.mapStyle`: Duel, Triangle, Corners, Ring, Lanes or Islands) changes where the start zones are. So **never write 800 or 400 into a unit**: use `world.width` / `world.height` for the size and `world.center` for the middle. `world.myCorner` is the centre of your start zone this round (on most maps it isn't a corner), and `world.safeZone` is the shrinking safe circle (section 6).

**Teams and allies.** Every unit has a `teamId`. Same `teamId` = friend, different = enemy. In free-for-all every player is their own team, so `allies` are just your own units. When the host picks teams (2v2, 3v3, 2v2v2, 4v4 …), `allies` also holds your **teammates'** units (their `ownerId` is not yours) and `enemies` holds only the other teams. You can't hurt allies: attacking one just idles. Your team wins together: everyone on the 1st team gets the 1st place points. Good team units help each other, e.g. a tank that walks next to a teammate's sniper.

## 4b. Drawing the unit: `sprite()`

**Always give every unit a `sprite()`**, so it shows up as pixel art in the battle. It is called once when the unit is checked (never during the battle) and must return the same thing every time (no randomness).

- `grid`: 8, 12, 16, 24 or 32. Every frame has exactly `grid` rows, and every row is exactly `grid` characters. 16 is a good default.
- `palette`: 1-16 entries. Each key is one character (not `.`), each value a `"#RRGGBB"` colour. `.` means transparent.
- `frames.idle` is required (1-4 frames). Optional: `walk` (1-4), `attack` (1-4), `hit` (exactly 1), `death` (1-4). At least 10% of the idle frame must be coloured in.
- The game scales the sprite to the unit's `size` (drawn `2 × size` px wide), so `grid` only sets how detailed it is. The game draws the team outline, the ground ring and the health bar, so don't draw those.

Tips:
- **Draw it facing right.** The game flips it when it walks or attacks to the left.
- **Keep a dark outline** around the shape so it pops on the floor.
- Use **2 walk frames** (legs apart, legs together) and **1 attack frame** (weapon out).
- The kid can ask for any look. **Describe it back to them first** ("A knight with a red cape and a big sword, facing right. Sound good?"), then draw it.
- Keep it friendly: no rude or scary pictures.
- If validation says `SPRITE_BAD_FRAME`, it gives the `state`, `frame`, `row` and `col` of the bad character: count carefully and fix that row.

Worked example: a 16 × 16 knight with a sword (it reuses rows, which is fine because the result is always the same):

```js
const PALETTE = {
  o: '#1B1B2F', // dark outline
  h: '#C8D3E0', // helmet shine
  a: '#8A9BB0', // armour
  s: '#F2C9A0', // face
  r: '#D83A3A', // red cape
  b: '#6B4226', // boots
  w: '#F4F8FF', // sword blade
};

// Head, body and sword: 13 rows of 16 characters each.
const BODY = [
  '................',
  '.....oooooo.....',
  '....ohhhhhao....',
  '....ohaaaaao....',
  '....oaoooooo....',
  '....oasssso.....',
  '....oaaaaaao....',
  '.....oooooo.....',
  '....orrrrrro..o.',
  '...orraaaarro.ow',
  '...oraaaaaaroowo',
  '...oraaaaaarowo.',
  '....oaaaaaaoo...',
];
// Body with the sword thrust forward (attack).
const STAB = [
  ...BODY.slice(0, 8),
  '....orrrrrro....',
  '...orraaaarrooo.',
  '...oraaaaaarowwo',
  '...oraaaaaarooo.',
  '....oaaaaaao....',
];
// Legs: 3 rows each.
const STAND = ['....obbo.obbo...', '....obbo.obbo...', '...ooooo.ooooo..'];
const STEP_OUT = ['...obbo...obbo..', '..obbo.....obbo.', '..oooo.....oooo.'];
const STEP_IN = ['.....obbobbo....', '.....obbobbo....', '....oooooooo....'];

export default class Knight {
  constructor() {
    this.name = 'Knight';
    // ...the other stats...
  }

  update(self, enemies, allies, world) {
    // ...
  }

  sprite() {
    return {
      grid: 16,
      palette: PALETTE,
      frames: {
        idle: [[...BODY, ...STAND]],
        walk: [[...BODY, ...STEP_OUT], [...BODY, ...STEP_IN]],
        attack: [[...STAB, ...STAND]],
      },
    };
  }
}
```

## 5. Cost

Every unit has a price in $. The game works it out from the stats. Each stat has a normal (linear) price, and the closer a stat gets to its max, the more extra it costs:

```
stat price = normal price × (1 + 2 × (stat / max)²)

  maxHealth: maxHealth × 0.25      (max 500)
  damage:    damage × 1.5          (max 60)
  range:     range × 0.2           (max 300)
  speed:     speed × 12            (max 5)
  size:      (size − 6) × 0.5      (max 20, so (size − 6) max 14)

cost = (10 + the five stat prices) × max(1, 60 / attackCooldown)
```

rounded **up** to whole dollars. If `size` is left out it is priced at the default (soldier 10, tower 14). The coefficients can be changed by the grown-ups during the event, so trust the `cost` and `breakdown` that `validate_unit` returns (or `get_rules`) over these numbers. The `breakdown.parts` list says, for each stat, how much is its normal price and how much is the extra for being near the max.

Worked examples:

- **Basic Soldier** (maxHealth 120, damage 10, range 40, speed 1.5, size 10, attackCooldown 60):
  maxHealth 30 × (1 + 2 × 0.24²) = 33.46, damage 15.83, range 8.28, speed 21.24, size 2.33.
  10 + 33.46 + 15.83 + 8.28 + 21.24 + 2.33 = 91.14, times max(1, 60/60) = 1 → **$92**.
- **Big Tank** (maxHealth 500, damage 20, range 40, speed 0.8, size 20, attackCooldown 60):
  maxHealth is at its max, so it costs 125 × 3 = 375 instead of 125. Size 20 is maxed too: 7 × 3 = 21.
  10 + 375 + 36.67 + 8.28 + 10.09 + 21 = 461.04 → **$462**. With maxHealth 250 instead it would be only **$180**.
- **Basic Tower** (maxHealth 150, damage 15, range 180, speed 0, size 14, attackCooldown 45): 148.09 × 60/45 → **$198**.
- **Speedy Scout** (maxHealth 60, damage 6, range 30, speed 4, size 6, attackCooldown 20): 150.17 × 3 → **$451**. With attackCooldown 10 it would be × 6 = $902: **too expensive** (max $500).

Tips:
- **Maxing a stat costs 3×: spread your stats.** A stat at its max costs 3 times its normal price, and one at half its max costs 1.5 times. Two medium units are cheaper than one giant one, and usually win. If a stat is at 80% of its max or more, `validate_unit` gives the tip `W_STAT_NEAR_MAX`.
- **Cooldown below 60 multiplies the whole cost.** Halving the cooldown doubles the price.
- **Cooldown above 60 gives no discount.** It costs the same as 60 but attacks less often, so use 60 instead of anything slower.
- Speed is pricey (12 per px/tick, more near the max). Range is cheap per pixel but adds up.
- A unit can cost at most **$500**. If it is too expensive, the error says which stat costs the most and how much lowering it saves: lower that one.

## 6. Budget and battle rules

- **$1,000 per round** to place units. Each copy placed costs the unit's price. Unspent money doesn't carry over.
- At most **3 unit types** per round, at most **12 copies** placed, at most **$500** per unit, and at most **8 designs** (revisions and the two starter templates don't count).
- **4-8 units is a good army.**
- Units can only be **submitted in the Plan phase**. You can validate at any time.
- A battle lasts 90 seconds. **Sudden death:** after 50 seconds a safe circle (`world.safeZone`, centred on `world.center`) shrinks toward the middle. Units outside it lose 7% of their max health every second, so armies are forced to meet. Smart units move inside `world.safeZone` late in the battle. These are the default numbers: `get_rules` (`suddenDeath`) has the exact ones if the host changes them.
- When time runs out, the player (or team) whose living units are worth the most money wins the round. Otherwise the last army (or team) standing wins.

### How points work

Every round, each player scores **place points + damage bonus + value bonus**:

- **Place points:** 1st gets (number of players − 1), then one less per place, down to 0 for last: with 4 players 3, 2, 1, 0; with 2 players 1, 0; with 8 players 7 down to 0 (players who tie share the better place). In team mode every member gets the team's place points.
- **Damage bonus (0-2 with 4 players):** `round(2 × players/4 × myDamage / topDamage)`, always your own (also in teams). Damage counts only what really hurts: a 40-damage hit on an enemy with 15 health left counts 15. Shots count when they land, even if the shooter is already gone.
- **Value bonus (0-1 with 4 players):** `round(1 × players/4 × myValueDestroyed / topValueDestroyed)`. When one of your hits knocks out an enemy unit, you score that unit's full price.
- Rounding is half-up (0.5 rounds up). Sudden-death damage counts for nobody.
- **Badges** (just for fun, no points): 🏆 Last Standing (1st place; in teams every member of the winning team), 💥 Biggest Hitter (most damage), 🗡️ Giant Slayer (most enemy value knocked out), 🛡️ Iron Wall (most army value still standing at the end).
- The match winner has the most points after all rounds; equal points are decided by total damage. In team mode there is also a team table (the average of the members' points).

**Strategy tip:** the bonuses are worth about as much as 1st place, so **aggressive units score damage points even if they don't survive**. An army that hits hard and comes 2nd can beat a turtle that hides and survives. A good mix is a few hard hitters to rack up damage plus something tough to stay standing. The exact numbers are in `get_rules` (`scoring`).
- There is no healing, no shields and no special powers: units can only move and attack. If the kid asks for something like that, explain it kindly and suggest the closest thing that works (e.g. a tough tank instead of a healer).

## 7. After a battle

Call `get_battle_report`. Its `score` shows how the kid's points were made (place, damage bonus, value bonus, badges): tell them in one cheerful sentence, e.g. "You came 2nd and your army dealt the most damage: +4 points and the Biggest Hitter badge!". If a unit threw errors, timed out, or got switched off, the report names the unit and the line. Fix that unit (pass its `unitId`), validate it, and submit it in the next Plan phase. Tell the kid what went wrong in simple words, e.g. "Your ninja got confused when there were no enemies left. I fixed it!"

### Open Armory (only when the host switched it on)

`get_rules` and `get_game_state` say whether Open Armory is on. Then every unit type that fought in a battle becomes borrowable by the other players from the next Plan phase, named like "Ogre (Ninja Sam)". Call `list_armory` to see them (stats, cost, round added). The kid picks borrowed units on the Pixel Clash page (they count toward the 3 types), and a borrowed unit's damage and points are the kid's. Borrowed units can't be changed and their code is never shared: `get_unit_source` on one says `NOT_FOUND`. Build your own counter or improved version instead (a new unit, submitted without `unitId`). When another kid fields one of *your* kid's units, your kid gets the 💡 Inventor badge.

## 8. Don'ts

- No `import`/`require`, network, timers, `async`, or `Math.random()` in the constructor or `sprite()` (the stats and the picture must be the same every time).
- Don't change stats inside `update()`: it has no effect.
- Don't call anything except the pixel-clash tools for the game. Don't browse the web or touch files outside this folder.
- Don't ask the kid to type code, edit files or run commands.

## 9. Fallback: if the pixel-clash tools are missing

If the `pixel-clash` MCP tools aren't available, use `curl` against the REST API with the apiKey from `.pixel-clash/player.json`. Write the literal apiKey into the command (not a shell variable). These commands are not pre-approved, so each one asks for permission: before the first one, tell the kid "Please ask a grown-up to come and check my request." Use only the commands below, exactly as written.

Log in (only if `.pixel-clash/player.json` is missing; then save `name` and `apiKey` there):

```sh
curl -sS -X POST http://{{HOST}}/api/login -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"name":"<NAME>","pin":"<PIN>"}'
```

Build the request body by writing the file `.pixel-clash/request.json` with your file-writing tool: a JSON object `{"source": "<the unit file's full text, JSON-escaped>"}` (add `"unitId": "<id>"` when revising). Then:

```sh
# Check (nothing stored):
curl -sS -X POST http://{{HOST}}/api/units/validate -H "Authorization: Bearer <API_KEY>" -H "Content-Type: application/json" --data-binary @.pixel-clash/request.json

# Submit (Plan phase only):
curl -sS -X POST http://{{HOST}}/api/units -H "Authorization: Bearer <API_KEY>" -H "Content-Type: application/json" --data-binary @.pixel-clash/request.json

# Game state and my units:
curl -sS http://{{HOST}}/api/state -H "Authorization: Bearer <API_KEY>"
curl -sS http://{{HOST}}/api/units -H "Authorization: Bearer <API_KEY>"
```

A failed check answers HTTP 422 with the same `errors` (each with a `fix`) as `validate_unit`. On Windows 10 or newer, use `curl.exe` instead of `curl`.
